# Host tools — agent buttons

- `publish_action` / `remove_action` (an agent's buttons, `ActionBroker` → `ActionPublishService`): append-only `action` / `action_outcome` rows, never edited. `ChatActionsService` is their one writer and holds the per-run lock every publish, press and dismissal takes; the live set is folded from the whole run (`utils/action-fold.ts`) and served as run state's `actions`, never folded by a client. An outcome counts only against the publish seq it names, so a press of a republished button is refused `409 ACTION_CHANGED`. An id is scoped to its publishing conversation; 20 live per run, the 21st refused. Labels refuse controls, format characters and line separators; the card and tooltip show a message's exact text before it is pressed. Press and dismiss (`ActionPressService`, chat routes `:runId/actions/:seq/press|dismiss`) carry no desktop-only guard; `via` comes from `cameViaRemote`. A message press on a busy chat answers the chat's own `RUN_BUSY` with nothing written, and the renderer presses the same seq again with the same `clientMessageId` when the turn settles.
  - Team members publish too (`TeamExecutorService.registerActionPublishers`, every call-capable member, dropped with the run): the publisher is built per call from the MCP `caller` — `conversationId` and the `callId` it answers (`CallBroker.callAnsweredBy`) sit inside `publisher`, never at the row's top level, so the rows stay in the run's own flow (`CALLER_FLOW_KINDS`). `agentLabel` is stamped on every publisher but a `lead`-mode run's lead in its own conversation (a press reaches whoever leads at the press, so in a `relay` run every publisher is labelled). A button published inside a call — or after it settled, starting from `CallBroker.callToNoteOn` as for pages — is named in that call's and every running ancestor's envelope under `actions` (`CallBroker.notePublishedAction`, noted after the live cap and before the write, undone if the row fails; a republish also refreshes every running call already naming its key, and a removal drops the key from every running call — `forgetPublishedAction`).
  - A stop action (`stop: {shell}` or `{pid}`) is resolved at publish by `ActionStopService` and always one-shot: a shell id (its `workId` or launching call id) names one of the publishing MEMBER's open `shell_open` rows (a member's conversations share them, so a refusal lists their ids, never their commands), whose launching call's command (`launchingCallOf`, never a delegate's call; an id naming shells of more than one conversation is refused) is matched (`commandTargets`) among the run's `RunProcessReaper.stopCandidates`; a command matching more than one separate process is refused, the agent pointed to `stop.pid`; a pid must itself be a candidate. Only the named process (or the command's top match) is recorded: what it starts is stopped at the press, never refused for having left the run, so a lost untagged worker cannot disable the button. Known limit: the command text is all that ties a shell to a process, so if the shell's own process has already exited while its row still reads open, a single other process whose command contains it is taken for it — the card shows its command line before the press. Never `ChatShellsService.kill`'s machine-wide match. Each target's `{pid, startedAt (ISO), command}` is recorded, the command one line, masked (`maskedCommand`: env assignment values and registered secrets). A press claims the button under the lock (`ACTION_BUSY` to a second press or a dismissal, held in memory so a daemon dying mid-stop leaves nothing pending), stops OUTSIDE the lock through `stopRecorded`, and records `stopped` (`alreadyStopped` when every target had exited) or `failed` with the refusal, which keeps the button live — unless the agent removed or republished the button meanwhile, when a failure answers `ACTION_GONE` / `ACTION_CHANGED` and writes nothing, so the removal or the newer publish stands.
  - A command action (`command: {argv, cwd?, timeout_minutes?, notify_agent?}`) is checked at publish: argv non-empty tokens with no control, invisible or bidi characters, at most `ACTION_COMMAND_MAX` characters; `cwd` an existing absolute directory, canonicalized, else the run's own folder (`ActionCommandService.resolveCwd`, run again at the press: a folder now leading elsewhere fails the run); 1–60 minutes, default 10, over 60 refused. The renderer runs nothing until the user confirms a dialog showing the exact argv and cwd (desktop and phone alike). A press writes `running` under the lock (a second press answers `ACTION_BUSY`), then runs OUTSIDE it through `runManagedCommand`: argv only, `buildChildEnv`, `runTagEnv`, `endGroupOnExit` (what the command backgrounded does not outlive it; a setsid'd process escapes to the run-tag reaper) and a settle on the exit after `EXIT_DRAIN_MS` even when a descendant holds the pipes. The output tail (both streams in arrival order) is masked (`redactSecrets`) before it is cut to `ACTION_OUTPUT_TAIL_MAX`. The finish row (`succeeded` / `failed` with `exitCode`, `outputTail`, `error`) is written whenever the run still exists, also after the agent removed or replaced the button (the fold never revives a retired publish). Each running command holds a slot in `ActionCommandRegistry`: `RunTeardownService.endProcesses` aborts them (archive and delete), a slot started within `ABORTED_RUN_MS` of that starts already aborted (`RunTeardownService.resumeProcesses` lifts it on unarchive), and `beforeApplicationShutdown` aborts every slot, and any started later, ahead of `ProcessRegistry`'s drain. A press reaching the lock while the run's commands are being ended is refused `RUN_ARCHIVED`; otherwise its slot is started before the `running` row is written. An aborted command ends `interrupted` and the agent is not told; `purge` waits (bounded) for its last row. `interrupted` retires a one-shot button only; a reusable one stays to be run again. `notify_agent` then delivers `[Action "<label>" finished: exit N]` and the last `ACTION_NOTIFY_TAIL_MAX` of output inside a fence tagged with a random id per message (`<command-output-<id>>`, which the output cannot print), after a line saying it is the command's output and not the user's words, through the press's own delivery (a team's goes to the run's lead), re-sent under one `clientMessageId` every `ACTION_NOTIFY_RETRY_MS` while the run answers `RUN_BUSY`, dropped on any other refusal, and lost if the daemon stops first. `ActionOutcomeSweep` (boot, after `reconcileStrandedShells`) writes `interrupted` for every publish whose newest outcome is `running` — live, removed or replaced — that a prior daemon left; its child was ended by the stranded-child reaper.
  - A team press (`TeamActionsService`, `POST /v1/teams/runs/:runId/actions/:seq/press|dismiss`) delivers through `TeamExecutorService.sendMessage` — to the run's lead (the trigger member in `lead` mode), never the publisher's own conversation — prefixed `From <agentLabel> (action "<label>"):` when the publisher carries a label. The chat routes refuse a team run (`NOT_A_CHAT_RUN`) and these a chat run (`NOT_A_TEAM_RUN`), checked in `ActionPressService.assertPressable` by the route's `ActionRunKind`.
